  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-two-level-full-factorial-design": [
      "## Definition\n\nFix an integer $k\\ge1$ and write $[k]=\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$. A **two-level full factorial design** has run set\n$$\\mathcal X_k=\\{-1,+1\\}^{[k]},$$\nso every one of the $2^k$ sign combinations occurs once. For a run $x\\in\\mathcal X_k$ and a subset $A\\subseteq[k]$, define its sign column by\n$$x_A=\\prod_{j\\in A}x_j,$$\nwhere the empty product is $x_\\varnothing=1$. The design matrix $X$ has one row for each run, one column for each subset of factors, and entry $X_{x,A}=x_A$. Any fixed ordering of the runs and subsets may be used.\n\nThe run set and subset index set both have $2^k$ elements: a subset $A$ corresponds to the sign vector whose $j$th coordinate is $+1$ exactly when $j\\in A$. Thus $X$ is a square $2^k\\times2^k$ matrix.\n\nFor a real response table $y=(y_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X_k}$, define its normalized factorial coefficient by\n$$\\widehat\\beta_A(y)=2^{-k}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X_k}x_Ay_x.$$\nFor nonempty $A$, put $G_{A,+}=\\{x:x_A=+1\\}$ and $G_{A,-}=\\{x:x_A=-1\\}$. Both sets are nonempty: the all-positive run lies in $G_{A,+}$, and flipping any coordinate in $A$ gives a run in $G_{A,-}$. Define the factorial effect on this response scale as\n$$\\widehat\\tau_A(y)=\\frac{1}{|G_{A,+}|}\\sum_{x\\in G_{A,+}}y_x-\\frac{1}{|G_{A,-}|}\\sum_{x\\in G_{A,-}}y_x.$$"
    ],
    "thm-full-two-level-factorial-contrast-orthogonality": [
      "## Statement\n\nFor the design in [[def-two-level-full-factorial-design]], let $c_A=(x_A(x))_{x\\in\\mathcal X_k}$ be the column indexed by $A\\subseteq[k]$. Then\n$$\\langle c_A,c_B\\rangle=\\begin{cases}2^k,&A=B,\\\\0,&A\\ne B.\\end{cases}$$\nThus the columns are pairwise orthogonal and\n$$X^{\\mathsf T}X=2^kI_{2^k},\\qquad 2^{-k}X^{\\mathsf T}X=I_{2^k}.$$\nThis is a deterministic property of the design matrix."
    ],
    "thm-full-factorial-estimator-and-effect-variances": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $r\\ge1$ be an integer. For every run $x\\in\\mathcal X_k$ and replicate $s\\in\\{1,\\ldots,r\\}$, suppose\n$$Y_{x,s}=\\sum_{B\\subseteq[k]}\\beta_Bx_B+\\epsilon_{x,s},$$\nwhere the coefficients $\\beta_B$ are fixed and the errors are mutually independent real random variables on one probability space, with $\\mathbb E[\\epsilon_{x,s}^2]<\\infty$ and common variance $\\sigma^2<\\infty$. Define $\\overline Y_x=r^{-1}\\sum_{s=1}^rY_{x,s}$ and\n$$\\widehat\\beta_A=2^{-k}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X_k}x_A\\overline Y_x.$$\nThen for every $A\\subseteq[k]$,\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(\\widehat\\beta_A)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{r2^k}.$$\nFor nonempty $A$, define $\\widehat\\tau_A$ as the difference of the mean run responses $\\overline Y_x$ over $G_{A,+}$ and $G_{A,-}$ from [[def-two-level-full-factorial-design]]. Then\n$$\\widehat\\tau_A=2\\widehat\\beta_A,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\widehat\\tau_A)=\\frac{4\\sigma^2}{r2^k}.$$\n\nThe variance calculation assumes equal replication at every run and independence across all run-replicate errors. It requires neither Gaussian errors nor zero interactions. A zero-mean assumption is additionally needed if $\\beta_B$ is to denote coefficients of the mean response; it is not needed for these variance identities."
    ],
